All 8 operations are implemented and verified. Here's what I implemented:

| Function | Description |
|----------|-------------|
| `append(list1, list2)` | Concatenates two lists by iterating through both |
| `concat(lists)` | Flattens a list of lists into a single list |
| `filter(function, list)` | Returns items where `function(item)` is True |
| `length(list)` | Counts items using a manual loop |
| `map(function, list)` | Applies function to each item |
| `foldl(function, list, initial)` | Reduces from left: `function(acc, item)` |
| `foldr(function, list, initial)` | Reduces from right: `function(item, acc)` |
| `reverse(list)` | Returns items in reverse order |

All implementations avoid built-in functions like `len()`, `sum()`, `reversed()`, etc. — using only manual iteration and list concatenation. Verified with 20+ test cases covering normal cases, edge cases (empty lists, single elements), and foldl/foldr argument ordering.
